#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ab3811-6a07-5a4f-add2-e6b77d629de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248248} {:answer/id #uuid "83f173fd-069b-5c9c-869c-55c841e6cefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248249} {:answer/id #uuid "83ccd592-0eb8-596d-b228-b145e11c6733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248250} {:answer/id #uuid "084141c9-1513-5913-badf-3b9fba939173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248251} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3adcb4-4e72-5d1e-9a29-ded4717d4688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83ccd592-0eb8-596d-b228-b145e11c6733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248250} :answer-field/id #uuid "56271f5b-d065-552d-856d-39f01005ecdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248247}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce3343a2-d1df-5e29-9c1e-5eef545143ce" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-119308" :question/problem "The point $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3+1)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(4))=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-4+4)=(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2)-1=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+4 \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f2ab7b5-528c-58c3-be91-a207f44a9759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({a}^{3/2},b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248255} {:answer/id #uuid "bad55f06-3843-5574-ab71-878a021ef5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248256} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1c1204-91f2-519d-a749-58f02360978f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248257} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b700ae-4927-54aa-b80b-3bc0afb645d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248258} {:answer/id #uuid "919b3f97-d545-5b10-9192-e89f46a84e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bad55f06-3843-5574-ab71-878a021ef5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248256} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8c4a621-a630-5830-991d-70bbbdcb2d6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248254}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e5ec0ec-aaa2-5e08-ad0e-3766733b956d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121399" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={3}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={3}^{x/2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of  $y={3}^{x/2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit up and obtain the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b+1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={3}^{x}$, we have that ${3}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ Substituting $x=2a$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={3}^{(2a)/2}+1 \\\\  & ={3}^{a}+1 \\\\  & =b+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2a,b+1)$ must lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e3c0425-09da-580e-aa4c-e064c229f5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248262} {:answer/id #uuid "cae78d90-5f49-51ff-afce-6b5b920b2dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248263} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf6bded-cc96-5f87-a92f-47b712a8feed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248264} {:answer/id #uuid "a91a2e2b-b792-590a-aab1-c455179de4c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248265} {:answer/id #uuid "c2235e17-6024-5f14-b6fe-f997300f66b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2235e17-6024-5f14-b6fe-f997300f66b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248266} :answer-field/id #uuid "e235d520-5d37-5a17-961e-b18d35afce18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248261}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46f4f815-755e-5113-8810-3f6c3fca180d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-211462" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939872/assets/8000655c1943c60282d8844a2ae965b6415e21962c44309cd68806f04744b806.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis,\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $3$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **maximum** of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(2x)+3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2,3)=(1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 3)=(1,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $3$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-3+3)=(1,0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3$ is $(1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78b0256c-0e68-5f58-92f5-a72c97ffd392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248269} {:answer/id #uuid "034acc57-e66f-5087-9e73-f22dec96af0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248270} {:answer/id #uuid "b454e047-1b1b-5ec7-893d-4c30be6c733e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248271} {:answer/id #uuid "14fc3536-0b20-5c0e-8663-58d0fb7df198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248272} {:answer/id #uuid "65031501-8ce8-51b9-aa46-4827220f34ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b454e047-1b1b-5ec7-893d-4c30be6c733e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248271} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8f76cf-077e-5170-9371-807adad5915d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248268}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c15e2d7c-d9c3-527e-9d7f-4dea7220cf07" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-211465" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939872/assets/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local minimum of \n$y=-f(x-1)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the right, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $2$ units down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **minimum** of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x-1)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+1,3)=(3,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1\\cdot 3)=(3,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-3-2)=(3,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local minimum of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ is $(3,-5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f5dd4e-4368-5186-a48f-e8762b711dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248276} {:answer/id #uuid "ab691808-d354-5149-a8aa-fc1fe4b55ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248277} {:answer/id #uuid "02015387-ce6e-5c24-b647-1861ff1dfa0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248278} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a16458-1e45-5f75-8e82-8bf48ff44f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248279} {:answer/id #uuid "c386d06e-caf9-5e07-ad74-6b30f76ae4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab691808-d354-5149-a8aa-fc1fe4b55ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248277} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d628707-448a-5d9d-94b4-62145e633e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248275}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8799f4ab-90b6-51ef-ae7f-9c9ea4335c76" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-26726" :question/problem "The point  $(4,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:  \n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,2-2)=(4,0).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,0)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(0))=(4,0).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,0)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,0+4)=(4,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(4)+2=2\\,⟹\\,f(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+4 \\\\  & =-0+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"}]}
